 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Precision:** Normalized inverse depth calculation requires careful handling of near/far plane bounds to avoid division by zero or numerical instability [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** Low-resolution grid sampling in Stage 1 (CT) may miss fine details if the grid is too coarse, requiring careful balancing of resolution vs. speed [S1].
-*   **Gradient Conflicts:** Freezing parameters in Stage 2 is critical; if geometry is not frozen in inverse rendering, material optimization may distort the shape [S2].
+*   **Instability:** Directly training with a quantized bottleneck (CodecSplat) is unstable; the two-stage separation is required to stabilize Gaussian generation [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Parameter Freezing:** In GS-IR, failing to freeze geometry and appearance in Stage 2 can lead to suboptimal material decomposition [S2].
+*   **Depth Normalization:** In CodecSplat, the normalization of inverse depth bounds ($n_i, f_i$) must be consistent between encoder and decoder to ensure $D_{codec-depth}$ is meaningful [S3, unofficial].
 
